window.docContents[248].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Nontrivial-finite-groups-of-odd-order-have-non-real-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Exercise5___1___7":{"contents":"Exercise 5.1.7. Show that any nontrivial finite group of odd order has an irreducible representation which is not defined over \\mathbb{R} (i.e., not realizable by real matrices).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.1. Frobenius-Schur indicator\tNontrivial finite groups of odd order have non-real irreducible representations","header":"Nontrivial finite groups of odd order have non-real irreducible representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Nontrivial-finite-groups-of-odd-order-have-non-real-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Exercise5___1___7"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___4___-Complementary-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_complementary_series_summary":{"contents":"We have now shown that for any \\nu with \\nu^q \\neq \\nu the representation Y_\\nu with the same character as\n\nW_1 \\otimes V_{\\alpha,1} - V_{\\alpha,1} - \\operatorname{Ind}_K^G \\mathbb{C}_\\nu\n\nexists and is irreducible. These characters are distinct for distinct pairs (\\alpha, \\nu) (up to switching \\nu \\to \\nu^q), so there are \\frac{q(q-1)}{2} such representations, each of dimension q - 1. These representations are called complementary series representations.\n\nWe have thus found q - 1 1-dimensional representations of G, \\frac{q(q-1)}{2} principal series representations, and \\frac{q(q-1)}{2} complementary series representations, for a total of q^2 - 1 representations, i.e., the number of conjugacy classes in G. This implies that we have in fact found all irreducible representations of GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\t5.25.4. Complementary series\tSummary: all q^2-1 irreducible representations of GL_2(F_q) found","header":"Summary: all q^2-1 irreducible representations of GL_2(F_q) found","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___4___-Complementary-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_complementary_series_summary"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Hook-length-formula___-dim-V_lambda-___-n___-___-product-of-hook-lengths/#Chapter5___Theorem5___17___1":{"contents":"Theorem 5.17.1 (The hook length formula). One has\n\n\\dim V_\\lambda = \\frac{n!}{\\prod_{(i,j): i \\leq \\lambda_j} h(i, j)}.\n\nProof. The formula follows from formula (5.17.1). Namely, note that\n\n\\frac{l_1!}{\\prod_{1 < j \\leq N} (l_1 - l_j)} = \\prod_{1 \\leq k \\leq l_1, k \\neq l_1 - l_j} k.\n\nIt is easy to see that the factors in this product are exactly the hook lengths h(i, 1). Now delete the first row of the diagram and proceed by induction. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.17. The hook length formula\tHook length formula: dim V_lambda = n! / product of hook lengths","header":"Hook length formula: dim V_lambda = n! / product of hook lengths","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Hook-length-formula___-dim-V_lambda-___-n___-___-product-of-hook-lengths/#Chapter5___Theorem5___17___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-via-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___12___2":{"contents":"The irreducible representations of S_n are described by the following theorem.\n\nTheorem 5.12.2. The subspace V_\\lambda := \\mathbb{C}[S_n] c_\\lambda of \\mathbb{C}[S_n] is an irreducible representation of S_n under left multiplication. Every irreducible representation of S_n is isomorphic to V_\\lambda for a unique \\lambda.\n\nThe modules V_\\lambda are called the Specht modules.\n\nThe proof of this theorem is given in the next subsection.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.12. Representations of $S_n$\tClassification of irreducible representations of S_n via Specht modules V_lambda","header":"Classification of irreducible representations of S_n via Specht modules V_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-via-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___12___2"}});
